<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xerxes I</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xerxes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xerxes I was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from 486 to 465 BCE, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. He led the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE, which won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> but was defeated at Salamis and, in 479 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. Accounts of the Achaemenid Empire discuss him in the context of the empire's military history and the limits of its westward expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darafsh · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
